canvasplayer
------------

Graffiti Markup Language (GML) playback in the browser, on HTML5 <canvas>,
with no dependencies.

Online demo: https://jamiew.github.io/canvasplayer
Code: https://github.com/jamiew/canvasplayer

GML data comes from #000000book, an open database of GML files:
https://000000book.com/data/147.json
https://000000book.com/data/147.gml
https://000000book.com/data/147


Using it
--------

    <div class="stage"><canvas id="canvas"></canvas></div>
    <script src="gml-player.js"></script>
    <script src="gml-source.js"></script>
    <script>
      GmlSource.load(147, function (tag) {
        new GmlPlayer(document.getElementById('canvas'), tag).play();
      });
    </script>

The canvas sizes itself to its parent, so give that element the dimensions you
want. gml-player.js has no dependencies and does not care where its data came
from -- feed it the same shape from anywhere:

    { id, app, screen: {x, y}, rotate, strokes: [ {points: [[x, y, time], ...]} ] }

gml-source.js is only the #000000book adapter: it fetches a tag and flattens
the GML tree into that. It tries fetch() and falls back to JSONP, because the
API sends no CORS headers yet.


What it does
------------

Plays a tag back in real time from the timestamps the capture app recorded,
repairing the dead time between strokes and saying so when it has had to.
Stroke width follows the speed of the hand, tapering the way a marker does.
Ink pools where the pen dwelled and runs from there.

Six drawing modes -- marker, hairline, outline, dots, spray, skeleton -- and
five effects: glow, ghost, chromatic, jitter, fade. A debug overlay draws the
captured points, velocity vectors, the bounding box and a speed graph, which
is a good way to see what a GML file actually contains.


What changed in v4
------------------

The 2009 version drew through Processing.js. This one does not, and fixes the
maths it inherited:

* playback advanced at most one point per frame, so a dense tag silently fell
  behind its own timeline. That is what the downsampling hack worked around
* stroke weight came from `sqrt(pow(dimx,2), pow(dimy,2))`. sqrt takes one
  argument, so the vertical component was discarded and width only ever
  tracked horizontal speed
* landscape captures were rotated by `rotate(80)`, which Processing reads as
  80 radians, not 80 degrees
* the canvas was a fixed 800x580, so taller tags were cropped
* normalized x and y were mapped straight onto canvas width and height. GML
  normalizes the two axes independently, so the capture screen's aspect has to
  be reapplied or tags come out squashed

Orientation now comes from <environment><up> in the GML, which records it per
capture -- the same app appears both ways. Tags older than roughly #200 have
no <environment> at all, and only those fall back to matching the client name.


Tests
-----

    node gml-player.test.js
    node gml-source.test.js


